-mixed-messages-carina.jpg","pageCount":213,"readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"crossover","narrativeScore":0,"accessibilityScore":0.171,"scholarlyScore":0,"traits":[{"id":"essayistic","score":1,"confidence":"high","evidence":["catalog text contains \"essay\"","catalog text contains \"essays\"","catalog text contains \"criticism\"","catalog text contains \"reflections\"","literature/criticism subject or topic","criticism award category"]},{"id":"popular","score":0.264,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"popular\"","major-award recognition"]},{"id":"accessible","score":0.18,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"popular\""]},{"id":"argument_driven","score":0.18,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"theory\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"“The girl” offered by movies, TV, magazines, and ads functions as a shifting ideal that shapes identities, teaching the author to think about herself and the world—especially what it means to raise a daughter amid these reflections.","confidence":0.62},"academicOrientation":{"score":35,"confidence":0.45},"profileConfidence":0.63,"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"f93b2faa660e62938d32dcdf0e2550a3fb8b49624db7dba0f7d9a66a8c2ed73a","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Gender & Sexuality","secondarySubjects":["Society & Culture"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily explores themes of gender representation and identity in popular culture, positioning itself within the discourse of gender and sexuality.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"3f916cbda7146b7173124a722711afbdf4bc0059d92ecc8a94ba4892adc9c7c7","summary":"\"Who is \"the girl\"? Look to movies, TV shows, magazines, and ads and the message is both clear and not: she is a sexed-up sidekick, a princess waiting to be saved, a morally infallible angel with no opinions of her own. She's whatever the hero needs her to be in order to become himself. She's an abstraction, an ideal, a standard, a mercurial phantom. In You Play the Girl, Chocano blends formative personal stories with insightful and emotionally powerful analysis. Moving from Bugs Bunny to Playboy Bunnies, Flashdance to Frozen, the progressive '70s through the backlash '80s, the glib '90s, and the pornified aughts--and at stops in between--she explains how growing up in the shadow of \"the girl\" taught her to think about herself and the world and what it means to raise a daughter in the face of these contorted reflections. In the tradition of Roxane Gay, Rebecca Solnit, and Susan Sontag, Chocano brilliantly shows that our identities are more fluid than we think, and certainly more complex than anything we see on any kind of screen.\"--Page 4 of cover.","subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Women in popular culture","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-you-play-the-girl-on-playboy-bunnies-stepford-wives-train-wrecks-and-other-mixed-messages-carina"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Sex role","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-you-play-the-girl-on-playboy-bunnies-stepford-wives-train-wrecks-and-other-mixed-messages-carina"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Women in mass media","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-you-play-the-girl-on-playboy-bunnies-stepford-wives-train-wrecks-and-other-mixed-messages-carina"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Sexism","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-you-play-the-girl-on-playboy-bunnies-stepford-wives-train-wrecks-and-other-mixed-messages-carina"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Social conditions","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-you-play-the-girl-on-playbo